```

Scheduling the same name twice panics at mount time, same shape as routes
and jobs:

```rust
use charpente::http::{App, AppConfig};
use charpente::kernel::JobError;
use charpente::prod::{ProdDisk, ProdEntropy};
use charpente::{Schema, Tx, job};
use std::time::Duration;

#[job(global, retain_dead)]
struct Tick {}

impl Tick {
    fn run(self, _tx: &mut Tx) -> Result<(), JobError> {
        Ok(())
    }
}

// "cron schedule \"nightly\" scheduled twice — schedule each name once"
fn build_app(data_dir: &str) -> std::io::Result<App> {
    Ok(App::open(
        Box::new(ProdDisk::new(data_dir)?),
        Schema::new(),
        Box::new(ProdEntropy::new()?),
        AppConfig::default(),
    )?
    .schedule("nightly", Duration::from_secs(3600), || Tick {})
    .schedule("nightly", Duration::from_secs(7200), || Tick {}))
}
```

A zero interval panics the same way, at kernel startup, with `"cron schedule
\"<name>\": interval is zero — a schedule needs an interval of at least 1ns; pick
the period it should run at"`.

An interval that overflows u64 nanoseconds (~584 years — not a realistic
schedule, but checked) panics too, with `"cron schedule \"<name>\":
interval <duration> exceeds u64 nanos (~584 years) — pick a smaller
interval"` — but unlike the duplicate-name panic, not synchronously at the
`.schedule(...)` call: `App::schedule` stores the `Duration` unconverted,
and the conversion only happens at kernel startup. For a `serve()`-based
app that startup runs on the spawned kernel thread, so `serve` has already
returned `Ok` when the panic takes the kernel down.

A sweep that acts in every tenant, as the introduction describes: the schedule enqueues a
`global, cross_tenant` job, and that job fans a `scoped_by` job out into each tenant it
names.

```rust
use charpente::auth::Principal;
use charpente::http::{ApiError, App, AppConfig};
use charpente::kernel::JobError;
use charpente::prod::{ProdDisk, ProdEntropy};
use charpente::{CrossTenant, Id, Schema, ScopedTx, Tenant, Tx, job, jobs, table};
use std::time::Duration;

#[table(global)]
struct Org {
    id: Id<Org>,
    name: String,
}

impl Tenant for Org {
    const NAME: &'static str = "Org";
    type Who = Principal;
    type Error = ApiError;
    fn authorize(_tx: &Tx, _who: &Principal, _scope: Id<Org>) -> Result<(), ApiError> {
        // Never asked by the sweep: a job has no principal.
        Err(ApiError::Unauthorized)
    }
}

#[table(scoped_by = Org)]
struct Window {
    id: Id<Window>,
    applied: bool,
}

/// Acts inside one org, the one the sweep named when it fanned this out.
#[job(scoped_by = Org, retain_dead)]
struct ApplyWindow {
    window: u64,
}

impl ApplyWindow {
    fn run(self, tx: &mut ScopedTx<'_, '_, Org>) -> Result<(), JobError> {
        if let Some(mut w) = tx.get(Id::<Window>::from_raw(self.window))? {
            w.applied = true;
            tx.put(&w);
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

/// Reads every org's windows and fans one `ApplyWindow` into each window's org.
#[job(global, retain_dead, cross_tenant)]
struct MaintenanceSweep {}

impl MaintenanceSweep {
    fn run(self, tx: &mut Tx, cap: &CrossTenant) -> Result<(), JobError> {
        let windows = tx.across_tenants::<Org>(cap).scan::<Window>()?;
        for (org, window) in windows {
            if !window.applied {
                tx.enqueue_in(org, ApplyWindow { window: window.id.raw() }, cap);
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

fn build_app(data_dir: &str) -> std::io::Result<App> {
    Ok(App::open(
        Box::new(ProdDisk::new(data_dir)?),
        Schema::new().table::<Window>(),
        Box::new(ProdEntropy::new()?),
        AppConfig::default(),
    )?
    .mount_jobs(jobs![ApplyWindow, MaintenanceSweep])
    .schedule("maintenance-sweep", Duration::from_secs(3600), || {
        MaintenanceSweep {}
    }))
}
```

### Ticks are transactions

Nothing about a tick is special: `CronRow` (`id`, `#[index] name`,
`interval_nanos`, `next_due: WallTime`) is a platform-registered row like
`Session`, and a due tick opens a transaction that reads that row, advances
`next_due` to the schedule's next grid slot, and calls your `make()` job's `tx.enqueue` —
all committed together. A schedule's phase survives process restarts and
failovers: `next_due` is wall time, judged against the node's `Clock::wall`,
and the row is upserted (not reset) at startup — `App::open` only registers
the table; the upsert itself happens when the kernel starts. `interval_nanos`
is the interval the row's `next_due` was set with: a deploy that changes a
schedule's interval leaves the row as it is, and the next tick writes both.

### Catch-up fires once, never backfills

If the process is down across more than one whole interval — say an
hourly schedule misses five hours of ticks — the next tick still only
enqueues **one** job, and `next_due` jumps to the next grid slot after `now`: a single
step past the present, not five queued catch-up runs for the five missed
hours. Backfill is a deliberate non-goal.

One rule covers on-time, late and caught-up ticks: `next_due` becomes the next
slot on the schedule's grid, `next_due + interval * ((now - next_due) /
interval + 1)`. A tick at 125 s on a 10 s schedule due at 100 s lands on 130 s,
not 135 s, so lateness never drifts the phase.

### Clock errors

The wall time the kernel hands out (each request's `now`, a tick's, a retry's
`due_at`) never runs backwards within a node. When the system clock steps back,
the reading holds at the latest value already used, for as far back as
`CLOCK_TOLERANCE` (60 s, `charpente::core::io::CLOCK_TOLERANCE`, the skew OIDC
already allows). A clock that falls further behind stops the node by name, and a
stopped kernel ends the process:

<!-- pinned -->
```text
clock went backwards: this node's wall clock reads <wall>, <gap> behind <held>, the latest time it already used — more than the 60s charpente tolerates (CLOCK_TOLERANCE). The node stopped rather than extend sessions or delay schedules by that much. Fix the system clock (check NTP) and restart; if the clock is right, the earlier reading came from a clock that ran ahead, and the node runs again after <held minus 60s>.
```

The hold is bounded in time as well as in distance. A clock that has read at or
behind the held value for more than the tolerance of elapsed time (the process's
monotonic clock, counted from when the held value last rose) also stops the
node. That is how a stuck clock shows, since its reading never falls behind the
hold, and it also ends a step back the clock is slow to recover from:

<!-- pinned -->
```text
clock stalled: this node's wall clock has not advanced past <held>, the latest time it already used, for <elapsed> of elapsed time — more than the 60s charpente tolerates (CLOCK_TOLERANCE). The node stopped rather than hold sessions open and schedules still. Fix the system clock (check NTP) and restart.
```

So each hold episode, a step back or a freeze, extends sessions and delays ticks
by at most the tolerance before the node stops by name, and a clock that stops
advancing stops the node at the first read more than 60 s after it first read the value it is stuck at.
Two clock errors pass every check. A
clock that runs slow never reads behind the hold. Steps within the tolerance add
up: a clock that steps back 50 s, catches up to the time it held, and steps back
50 s again runs 100 s behind true time, since neither step exceeds the
tolerance and neither hold lasts longer than it. The node's own time stays
consistent through both, but it drifts against every clock it is compared with:
the identity provider's, which stamps an OIDC token's `exp` and `nbf`, and
another node's, which minted the sessions a follower or a promoted node judges.
Keep NTP on. It is what keeps a clock from wandering; the checks bound what one
bad step or a stopped clock can do.

A restart starts the hold over, so the same check runs whenever a node opens,
whatever its role (a follower judges session expiry on reads with its own clock,
and a primary held write-gated by its witnesses is opening too), and at a
promote. The node recovers the latest wall time its data directory recorded from
times it already stores: the newest API key's `created_at`, and each schedule's
`next_due - interval_nanos`, which is the time of its last tick or, before any,
of the open that created it. It refuses while its clock is more than the
tolerance behind that time, and otherwise starts its hold there:

<!-- pinned -->
```text
refusing to <verb>: this node's wall clock reads <wall>, <gap> behind <floor>, the latest time this data directory recorded — more than the 60s charpente tolerates (CLOCK_TOLERANCE). Fix the system clock (check NTP); if the clock is right, the recorded time came from a clock that ran ahead, and the node will <then> after <floor minus 60s>.
```

`<verb>` is `open` or `promote`, and `<then>` is what the node does once its
clock reaches the instant the text names. At open the refusal stops the kernel,
and `<then>` is `open`. A refused promote answers `CtrlErr` with the text before
the durable role flip, so the node stays a follower, and `<then>` is `promote`:
promote it again after that instant. A switchover's target cannot refuse the
hand-off (§10: once sent, there is no abort), so the switchover completes and
the target's kernel stops with `promote` as `<verb>` and `open` as `<then>`. Its
role flipped with the hand-off, so the restart that follows opens it as a
primary: until its clock reaches the same instant, that open refuses with `open`
in both places. No flag skips either check.

The recorded time is a lower bound, as recent as the last key minted or a
schedule's last tick or creation (the built-in session sweep ticks daily). A clock behind true time by
less than the time since then, plus the tolerance, opens unnoticed, and that is
also how long a session that expired just before a restart or a failover can
resolve again.

### Virtual time in tests

Because the simulator's clock is virtual, a schedule's real-world interval
costs nothing extra to exercise exhaustively: driving a monthly cron
schedule through ten simulated years (121 ticks) runs in well under a
second of wall-clock time. Nothing about cron's tick behavior depends on
wall-clock speed — §8 shows the machinery (a scripted kernel under virtual
time, in "Driving the whole app in a sim") and `charpente test --sim`, the
runner.

### `tx.now()`: the step's time inside a transaction

A route, a job, and a job a schedule enqueued can read the time of the step they run in:
`tx.now()` returns a `WallTime`, the reading the kernel held at the start of
that step (§7, "Clock errors"), the same value for every transaction the step
serves. The log records no time for it; a seed's replay reproduces the same
reading. `tx.now()` is reachable through a tenant-scoped transaction too, and
on a follower's read route it is the follower's own held reading.

The bound on how late a timed job runs comes from the node's clock check. A
node whose clock is behind by less than the time since the store's newest recorded time (an API key's
creation, or a schedule's last tick) plus `CLOCK_TOLERANCE` opens unnoticed.
For an app with no keys and no schedule of its own, that leaves the daily
session sweep: about 24 h plus 60 s. So a timed obligation built on `tx.now()`
can run late by up to that much, and never early in the node's own frame. A
frequent schedule tightens the bound: the store then holds a recent time, and
a clock behind it by more than the tolerance is refused at open.

`Db::transact` and `Db::begin_group` carry no time, and `tx.now()` in them
panics by name. `App::handle` carries no transaction time either, so a route's
`tx.now()` there panics by name, and `App::handle_at(request, now)` carries
one. A test gives a transaction one with `Db::transact_at(now, f)`,
`Group::set_now(now)` on a group from `Db::begin_group`, or
`App::transact_for_tests_at(now, f)`; `App::begin_group` takes the time as its
argument.

### Time-relative state: `tx.now()` and timed jobs

A transaction receives only `&mut Tx`; no `Clock` reaches inside it, because a
wall-clock read there is the ambient nondeterminism the platform exists to
remove. "Overdue" and every other time-relative rule is expressed with the two
things the platform does give you: `tx.now()` for a comparison, and a timed
enqueue (§6, "Due at a time") for an obligation that has to happen later.

**A hold that expires seven days after it is placed.** Enqueue the expiry
when the hold is placed, and let the job do the work when it is due:

```rust
use charpente::core::table::Id;
use charpente::http::ApiError;
use charpente::kernel::JobError;
use charpente::{Tx, View, job, route, table};
use std::time::Duration;

#[table(global)]
struct Hold {
    id: Id<Hold>,
    released: bool,
}

#[job(global, retain_dead)]
struct Expire {
    hold: u64,
}

impl Expire {
    fn run(self, tx: &mut Tx) -> Result<(), JobError> {
        let id = Id::<Hold>::from_raw(self.hold);
        if let Some(mut hold) = tx.get(id)? {
            hold.released = true;
            tx.put(&hold);
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

#[route(POST "/holds/:id", public)]
fn place_hold(tx: &mut Tx, id: Id<Hold>) -> Result<(), ApiError> {
    tx.put(&Hold { id, released: false });
    tx.enqueue_after(Duration::from_secs(7 * 24 * 3600), Expire { hold: id.raw() });
    Ok(())
}
```

The hold and its expiry commit in one transaction, so there is never a hold
without its expiry or an expiry without its hold. The job runs once the
dispatcher reaches seven days after the step that placed the hold, and a
kernel that was down at that moment runs it once on restart.

**Cancelling is declining.** There is no cancel API for a timed job, and none
is needed. A booking's reminder is an outbound job (§6), and its `call` reads
committed state when the job is due: it returns `Ok(None)` when the booking was
cancelled in the meantime, and a job whose `call` declines is Done, so nothing
retries it and nothing is sent:

```rust
use charpente::core::table::Id;
use charpente::kernel::JobError;
use charpente::outbound::{Call, IdempotencyKey, Reply};
use charpente::{Tx, View, job, table};

#[table(global)]
struct Booking {
    id: Id<Booking>,
    cancelled: bool,
}

#[job(global, retain_dead, outbound = "mail.example")]
struct Remind {
    booking: u64,
}

impl Remind {
    fn call(&self, tx: &Tx, key: &IdempotencyKey) -> Result<Option<Call>, JobError> {
        let Some(booking) = tx.get(Id::<Booking>::from_raw(self.booking))? else {
            return Ok(None);
        };
        if booking.cancelled {
            return Ok(None);
        }
        Ok(Some(
            Call::post("/remind").header("idempotency-key", key.as_str()),
        ))
    }

    fn settle(self, _tx: &mut Tx, _reply: Reply) -> Result<(), JobError> {
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

A pure job declines the same way by doing nothing: `Expire` above leaves a
hold alone that was already released. §6 covers `call` returning `Ok(None)`.

**Lateness.** A timed job runs at its due time or later, never early in the
node's own frame. How late is the bound `tx.now()` states above: a node whose
clock was behind at open can hold a job back by up to the time since the
store's newest recorded time plus `CLOCK_TOLERANCE`, and a job due while the
node was down runs once at recovery rather than once per missed moment.

**Recurring work.** `App::schedule`'s `make` closure takes no tick time. The
job it builds reads `tx.now()` when it runs, like any other job, so a nightly
sweep that needs "everything older than 30 days" computes the cutoff from
`tx.now()` and queries for it. An obligation tied to one row is a timed
enqueue, not a sweep. Cron's catch-up rule (above) still applies: a
tick after downtime runs the job once, never once per missed interval.

An app that needs a real-world timestamp on a row (an audit log callers expect
in UTC, say) stores `tx.now().as_nanos()` in it; a seed's replay reproduces the
same reading.

### Putting it together

Routes, sessions, jobs, and a cron schedule all mount onto the same `App`;
one call to `charpente::serve::serve` starts all of it:

```rust
use charpente::core::table::Id;
use charpente::http::{ApiError, App, AppConfig, Ctx};
use charpente::kernel::JobError;
use charpente::prod::{ProdDisk, ProdEntropy};
use charpente::{Schema, Tx, View, Wire, job, jobs, route, routes, table};
use serde::Deserialize;
use std::time::Duration;

#[table(global)]
struct Note {
    id: Id<Note>,
    text: String,
}

#[table(global)]
struct DigestState {
    id: Id<DigestState>,
    sent: u64,
}

#[derive(Wire, Deserialize)]
struct NoteBody {
    text: String,
}

#[job(global, retain_dead)]
struct SendDigest {}

impl SendDigest {
    fn run(self, tx: &mut Tx) -> Result<(), JobError> {
        let sent = tx.get(Id::<DigestState>::from_raw(0))?.map_or(0, |s| s.sent);
        tx.put(&DigestState {
            id: Id::from_raw(0),
            sent: sent + 1,
        });
        Ok(())
    }
}

#[route(POST "/notes/:id", public)]
fn create_note(tx: &mut Tx, id: Id<Note>, body: NoteBody, ctx: &Ctx) -> Result<Id<Note>, ApiError> {
    let _ = ctx;
    tx.put(&Note {
        id,
        text: body.text,
    });
    Ok(id)
}

#[route(GET "/notes/:id", public)]
fn get_note(tx: &mut Tx, id: Id<Note>) -> Result<String, ApiError> {
    Ok(tx.get(id)?.ok_or(ApiError::NotFound)?.text)
}

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let app = App::open(
        Box::new(ProdDisk::new("./data")?),
        Schema::new().table::<Note>().table::<DigestState>(),
        Box::new(ProdEntropy::new()?),
        AppConfig::default(),
    )?
    .mount(routes![create_note, get_note])
    .mount_jobs(jobs![SendDigest])
    .schedule("monthly-digest", Duration::from_secs(30 * 24 * 3600), || {
        SendDigest {}
    });

    std::process::exit(charpente::serve::serve("0.0.0.0:8080", app)?.wait());
}
```

A pure background worker — jobs and cron, no routes of its own — mounts
zero routes and still calls `serve`: v0's only entry point binds an HTTP
listener alongside the job/cron loop (unrouted requests just 404). There
is no separate headless-worker entry point yet.

## 8. Sim testing

Everything in §1–§7 routes ambient IO through `Disk`, `Entropy`, and
`Clock` instead of touching the OS directly — that is the whole reason
`charpente-sim` can exist. `charpente_sim::Sim` implements all three traits
against one seed: a `u64` that determines virtual time, every entropy draw,
and a fault matrix (write errors, sync errors, crashes between any two disk
operations, torn writes). The whole app is what the simulator drives:
requests, jobs and cron through the same kernel loop (§5–§7) production
runs — see "Driving the whole app in a
sim" below. Your handlers run completely unmodified; nothing about your app
becomes "testable" specially, because it was already built on the traits.
The product's core promise: the same seed always produces the same
run, so a failure a workload returns is not just a bug report — it is
a one-command reproduction, forever.

### `tests/sim.rs`

§1 already set up the pieces: `charpente-sim` as a `dev-dependency`, and a
`[[test]] name = "sim", harness = false` target. `harness = false` matters
here — it tells Cargo not to link the default libtest harness into that
binary, so `charpente_sim::sim_tests! { ... }` can supply its own `main`
instead, one that knows how to run many seeded runs and report on
them, rather than running a fixed list of `#[test]` functions once each.

A workload is a plain function, `fn(&mut Sim) -> Result<(), String>`: `Ok(())`
means the run passed; `Err(message)` means it didn't, and so does a
panic (both turn into the same kind of failure record). There is a third
ending — see "Giving up" below: a run the injected faults stopped before
your property could be adjudicated at all. Say so with `sim.give_up(reason, detail)`;
it is neither a pass nor a failure, and the report counts it separately.

it is neither a pass nor a failure, and the report counts it separately.
`sim_tests!` lists
the workloads to run by name, each optionally followed by `require [...]` —
sometimes-coverage keys (`Sim::cover(&self, what: &'static str)`, so a key is a
literal or a `const`, not a `format!`ed `String`; plus the sim's own auto-covered
`fault:*`/`crash:*`/`restart` keys) that must be hit at least once across
that test's runs in a batch run, or the batch fails even if every
run individually passed. Every run counts toward it, passed, skipped and
failed alike: a run that reached the state and then failed tested it. A
`require`d key that never fires is telling
you something real: either the workload never drives the system into that
state, or the run count is too low to make it likely.

```rust
use charpente::{Db, DbConfig, Id, Schema, TxError, View, table};
use charpente_sim::Sim;
use charpente_sim::checks::{boot_through_fallout, is_fault_fallout, replays_identically};

#[table(global)]
struct Counter {
    id: Id<Counter>,
    hits: u64,
}

// This is the shape of `tests/sim.rs`'s top level — the macro generates the
// binary's `fn main`, wiring these two workloads (and their `require` keys,
// if any) into the harness's arg parsing and reporting.
charpente_sim::sim_tests! {
    counter_roundtrip require ["restart"],
    counter_replays,
}

/// `sim_tests!` workloads return `Result<(), String>` — this one delegates
/// to a helper that returns the interesting value instead, so the
/// determinism checker below can reuse the exact same logic. `None` asserted
/// nothing, and a pass needs a verdict, so it gives the run up ("Giving
/// up" below).
fn counter_roundtrip(sim: &mut Sim) -> Result<(), String> {
    if counter_roundtrip_result(sim)?.is_none() {
        sim.give_up("nothing to assert: the write never acked, or a boot never came up", "");
    }
    Ok(())
}

/// `replays_identically` is the determinism oracle: it runs `workload` twice
/// on two fresh `Sim`s seeded identically and demands both the return value
/// and the durable end-state match byte-for-byte.
fn counter_replays(sim: &mut Sim) -> Result<(), String> {
    replays_identically(sim.seed(), counter_roundtrip_result)
}

/// Puts a row, restarts the simulated disk (crashes and reopens), and reads
/// it back. Returns `None` — not a failure — when there is nothing to
/// assert: an injected fault kept the write from its ack, or either
/// `Db::open` never booted through fault fallout (the harness's own fault
/// paths, not this workload's contract, are under test elsewhere). Only a
/// `TxError::Io` can be fallout: the closure's own `App` error, or a
/// follower's refusal, is this workload's bug. `is_fault_fallout` recognizes the
/// sim's own injected-fault wording, the WAL/engine poison wording, and
/// redb's "close and re-open the database" latch; `boot_through_fallout`
/// boots again on those, and excuses a refusal for durable corruption only
/// where the harness injected some. Anything else is treated as a real bug
/// and fails the run.
fn counter_roundtrip_result(sim: &mut Sim) -> Result<Option<u64>, String> {
    let schema = || Schema::new().table::<Counter>();
    let open = || Db::open(Box::new(sim.disk()), DbConfig::default(), schema());
    let Some(mut db) = boot_through_fallout(sim, 8, is_fault_fallout, open)
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?
    else {
        return Ok(None);
    };
    let acked = match db.transact(|tx| {
        tx.put(&Counter {
            id: Id::from_raw(1),
            hits: 1,
        });
        Ok::<_, std::io::Error>(())
    }) {
        Ok(()) => true,
        Err(TxError::Io(e)) if is_fault_fallout(&e.to_string()) => false,
        Err(e) => return Err(format!("{e:?}")),
    };

    sim.restart(); // power-cycles the simulated disk: unsynced writes tear or vanish
    drop(db); // the old handle is dead after restart — reopen, per its own contract

    let open = || Db::open(Box::new(sim.disk()), DbConfig::default(), schema());
    let Some(db) = boot_through_fallout(sim, 8, is_fault_fallout, open)
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?
    else {
        return Ok(None);
    };
    if !acked {
        return Ok(None);
    }
    let snap = db.snapshot().map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    match snap.get::<Counter>(Id::from_raw(1)).map_err(|e| e.to_string())? {
        Some(row) => Ok(Some(row.hits)),
        None => Err("acked Counter row lost across a crash".to_string()),
    }
}
```

### Examples to copy

Four compiled examples ship in the `charpente` crate. `examples/api.rs` is
the API page this guide opens with, read and never run: its `main` is empty.
The other three are each a small batch that prints the report "Reading a
batch" describes and exits with its code. Each is a whole `tests/sim.rs`:
`sim_tests!` supplies `main`, and the file declares its own tables, routes
and jobs.

- **In your app**, copy one of the three to `tests/sim.rs` and run
  `charpente test --sim`. It needs only the manifest `charpente new` writes.
- **In a charpente checkout**, run `cargo run -p charpente --example <name>`.
  Cargo refuses `-p charpente` anywhere else. The batch's last line names
  `charpente test --sim --master-seed 0x…`; for an example, pass the same flags
  after `--` instead: `cargo run -p charpente --example <name> -- --master-seed
  0x…` reruns that batch byte for byte.

| Example | File, in the `charpente` crate | It shows |
|---|---|---|
| `drawn_inputs` | `examples/drawn_inputs.rs` | bids drawn with `sim.input()` (`between`, `pick`, `shuffle`, `text`), a check that holds for any of them and merges the tail's re-sent answers into its judgement, and a `require` on a key the check marks when it judged something |
| `modelled_remote` | `examples/modelled_remote.rs` | an outbound job against a `Remote` with its own `Rng`, a calm switch flipped between the faulted phase and the tail, a route safe to receive twice, and the three outbound checkers |
| `restart_under_faults` | `examples/restart_under_faults.rs` | the recovery drive's four steps over a power cycle, a claim about the faulted phase's acks only, and a `require ["restart"]` |

All three drive the app with `charpente_sim::drive` ("A fault-free tail for an
end-of-run check" below). In all three a skipped run gives a constant `reason`
and puts the run's own text in the `detail`. The files are in the registry copy of the crate, under
`crates/charpente/examples/` in a checkout, and this guide is the
`charpente::guide` page on docs.rs.

### Drawing inputs

`sim.input()` is for the inputs a property must hold for: a name, an amount, an
order of operations. Each draw comes from a stream of its own, so drawing moves
no fault, and the harness records each draw's choice in a sequence it can replay
and shrink. The choice is the one the draw used: the stream's raw value reduced
into the draw's range, which is the smallest raw value that draws the same
value. So a choice and its value are one to one, and two equal values drawn
with the same bounds are equal choices.

```text
let i = sim.input();
let qty = i.between("qty", 1, 500);        // inclusive; shrinks toward 1
let kind = i.pick("kind", &["a", "b"]);    // shrinks toward the first item
i.shuffle("order", &mut ops);              // shrinks toward the given order
let note = i.text("note", &["x", "y"], 1, 4);
```

It is the workload's own script only: a modelled remote keeps its own `Rng`. A
drawn input tests how the app handles what its model stand-in returns, never
the real model's behaviour. `between` panics when `lo > hi`, and `pick` on an
empty slice. `text` panics when `min_words > max_words`, and on an empty
`words` when `max_words` is above 0; with a `max_words` of 0 it draws `""`.
Each message names the call and the draw, and says which argument to change.

When a check fails, its first failure is shrunk to smaller draws that still
fail it, and the failure block lists them by name on its `inputs:` line
("Shrinking a failure" below).

A loop's length decides how well it shrinks. When the length is a drawn count
(`let n = i.between("notes", 1, 4);` and then `n` items, the shape of the
scaffold's `tests/sim.rs` and `drawn_inputs.rs`), deleting one item leaves the
count stale: the loop reads the next item's draws in its place, the draws after
the loop shift, and the run usually passes, while lowering the count alone
drops the last item instead. So the shrink can keep items a smaller count would
drop. The failure still replays and is still the failure it was, but the shrunk
run may not be minimal. When the items after the first matter to a failure (a
later item, or a draw after the loop, depends on them), draw a "one more?"
choice before each item instead, so that deleting an item deletes exactly its
draws:

```text
let mut qtys = Vec::new();
while qtys.len() < 6 && *i.pick("more", &[false, true]) {  // shrinks toward stopping
    qtys.push(i.between("qty", 1, 500));
}
```

A batch counts, on each check's line, how many distinct inputs its tested runs
drew: two runs that drew the same values, under the same names and in the same
order, count once.
It is a count, never a threshold. A check whose workload draws nothing is named
above the check lines, because every one of its runs tested the same scripted
case, whatever the faults did ("Reading a batch" below).

### Giving up: a skipped run

Sooner or later a workload hits a run where the faults are severe enough
that it cannot test its claim at all: the database never opened, the write was
never acknowledged, every recovery attempt was exhausted, or durable corruption
cut the very record the claim was about. That is not a pass —
nothing was proven — and it is not a failure either; the harness did exactly
what it was told.

Say so:

```rust
use charpente::{Db, DbConfig, Id, Schema, TxError, View, table};
use charpente_sim::Sim;
use charpente_sim::checks::{boot_through_fallout, durable_corruption_fired, is_fault_fallout};

#[table(global)]
struct Counter {
    id: Id<Counter>,
    hits: u64,
}

fn counter_survives(sim: &mut Sim) -> Result<(), String> {
    let schema = || Schema::new().table::<Counter>();
    // A boot that meets an injected fault restarts and boots again, as a
    // supervised process would: `Db::open`'s contract for an IO failure.
    let open = || Db::open(Box::new(sim.disk()), DbConfig::default(), schema());
    let Some(mut db) = boot_through_fallout(sim, 8, is_fault_fallout, open)
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?
    else {
        // Nothing to adjudicate: the disk never let the claim become testable.
        sim.give_up("Db::open never booted in 8 boots", "");
        return Ok(());
    };
    let put = db.transact(|tx| {
        tx.put(&Counter {
            id: Id::from_raw(1),
            hits: 1,
        });
        Ok::<_, std::io::Error>(())
    });
    match put {
        Ok(()) => {}
        // An injected fault kept the write from its ack: no claim to check.
        // Any other error is the platform's, and fails, as the opens do.
        Err(TxError::Io(e)) if is_fault_fallout(&e.to_string()) => {
            sim.give_up("the write was never acknowledged, so there is no claim to check", e);
            return Ok(());
        }
        Err(e) => return Err(format!("{e:?}")),
    }

    sim.restart(); // a crash: unsynced writes tear or vanish
    drop(db); // the old handle is dead after restart
    let open = || Db::open(Box::new(sim.disk()), DbConfig::default(), schema());
    let Some(db) = boot_through_fallout(sim, 8, is_fault_fallout, open)
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?
    else {
        sim.give_up("the reopen never booted in 8 boots", "");
        return Ok(());
    };
    match db.snapshot().and_then(|snap| snap.get::<Counter>(Id::from_raw(1))) {
        Ok(Some(_)) => Ok(()),
        // The one loss the platform excuses on a single node, and an excused
        // run GIVES UP: the row's durability was not adjudicated, and a pass
        // needs a verdict.
        Ok(None) if durable_corruption_fired(sim) => {
            sim.give_up("durable corruption cut the acked row, so durability was not adjudicated", "");
            Ok(())
        }
        Ok(None) => Err("acked Counter row lost across a crash".to_string()),
        Err(e) if is_fault_fallout(&e.to_string()) => {
            sim.give_up("the read-back hit fault fallout", e);
            Ok(())
        }
        Err(e) => Err(e.to_string()),
    }
}
```

- `sim.give_up(reason, detail)` records the give-up **on the run**, not in
  the value you return. That is deliberate: a `Result` can be mapped back to
  `Ok(())` by a wrapper before the runner ever sees it, and for a long time
  that is exactly what happened — a property could report green having
  concluded nothing over most of its runs.
- **The reason is fixed text; the detail is this run's own.** `reason` is a
  `&'static str` that names the *kind* of give-up and embeds nothing that
  varies between runs: no error text, count or seq. A batch groups each
  check's skipped runs by reason and counts each group, so a reason with a seq
  in it makes every run its own group and the counts say nothing. What varies
  goes in `detail` (anything `Display`, such as the fallout error `e`): that one
  run's own text, which a batch does not print and `--seed` prints under the
  skipped check as `detail:`. A batch gives each reason the seed of its first
  run, `(first: --seed 0x…)`, so `charpente test --sim --filter <check> --seed
  0x…` replays that run and prints its detail, then a `fired:` line: what
  fired in that run, rendered as a failure block's `fired:` line is (less its
  own `run:skipped`), the line to reason from about why it gave up. A
  constant such as a retry count is fixed text, so it belongs in the reason
  (`"Db::open never booted in 8 boots"`), not the detail. A give-up with
  nothing to add passes `""`, and an empty detail is none.
  `sim.gave_up()` reads the reason and `sim.gave_up_detail()` the detail;
  `Outcome::GaveUp` carries both.
- First call wins, reason and detail together; later ones are consequences of
  the first.
- **Give up and stop.** The record is sticky and nothing enforces terminality,
  so if you give up inside a retry arm and the loop then goes on to adjudicate,
  the run is still reported as skipped and that verdict is thrown away.
  `give_up` and the `return`/`break` belong on the same arm. This bites hardest
  in a multi-round checker on your own `sim` (`acked_implies_durable`).
- A workload that gives up and then returns `Err` is still a **failure**.
  Failures outrank give-ups.
- It also marks the coverage key `run:skipped` — once per run, not
  once per call — so a give-up *rate* is counted like any other sometimes-key,
  and it cross-checks the reported count exactly. A run that gives up and
  then returns `Err` is counted as failed, so `charpente test --sim` counts
  every key it reached, toward `require` and in the JSON's `coverage`, except
  its `run:skipped`. `ceiling:
  [("run:skipped", N)]` on `run_universes_swarm!`/`run_universes_swarm_with!`
  caps it: the batch fails when more than N runs gave up, naming the key,
  the count and the cap. Twenty of the platform's own swarm properties carry
  one:
  - kernel's exactly-once and job-tenancy properties;
  - the flagship properties of tenant, transactions, http, group_commit and
    typed_tables, and archive's two convergence properties;
  - realtime's flagship, scoped settled-client and tenant-feed properties,
    migrations' two, and the WAL suite's fault-matrix, bit-flip, misdirected-write, read-error,
    dir-entry and gc properties.

  The rest carry none yet, so a drift in their give-up rates is visible only in
  the split: realtime's mixed-shapes and follower properties, replication's,
  auth's, kernel's job-shape property, transactions' crashed-reseed property,
  and engine's. A give-up is
  reported and never failed, so without a cap a property can drift toward
  adjudicating nothing with every batch green; a floor on the key would bound
  give-ups from below, which guards nothing. An app's `sim_tests!` takes no
  `ceiling:`. Its property `require`s instead the key its verdict marks, past
  every give-up arm. That fails a batch in which no run reached a verdict,
  but not one whose rate merely drifted.
- A cap bounds what a property fails to conclude, not how much it checks, so
  pair it with a `floor: [("key", N)]` on the key that counts its checks
  (written before `ceiling:`, on either macro). A change that skips a round's
  work without giving up meets every cap. At a pinned base the floor is the
  exact count; at an ambient one it is a margin, the mean over ten or more
  fresh batches less four standard deviations (§8).
- If your workload already funnels give-ups through a wrapper (the
  `Err("universe-inconclusive: …")` idiom the platform's own suites use), put
  the `sim.give_up(INCONCLUSIVE, e)` call there, beside the `sim.cover(...)` mark. Keep
  both: the coverage keys are a floorable distribution over *causes*, and the
  outcome is the scalar the runner reports. Neither replaces the other.

Every batch states, check by check, how many runs tested the check and, when any
were skipped, how many and why. A check with nothing skipped shows no skipped
count and is left out of the reasons block, and a batch that skipped nothing
prints no reasons block at all. This is a pinned batch of `crates/sim-fixture`,
`charpente test --sim --runs 64 --master-seed 0x5eed --filter counter_roundtrip`:

<!-- pinned -->
```text
skipped runs hit a fault before reaching the check, so they prove nothing either way:
  counter_roundtrip
    10  Db::open after the crash never booted in 8 boots (first: --seed 0x553274161bbf8475)
     2  the write was never acked, so durability has nothing to prove (first: --seed 0x70d29b6c7d22528d)

these checks draw no inputs, so every run tested the same case — draw them with sim.input() (GUIDE §8):
  counter_roundtrip

check counter_roundtrip ... ok  (52 tested, 12 skipped)

sim result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed. Runs tested: 52 of 64.
rerun exactly this batch: charpente test --sim --master-seed 0x5eed --runs 64 --filter counter_roundtrip
```

A check in which every run was skipped is `untested`, never `ok`, because a
property that adjudicated nothing is vacuous no matter how green it looks, and
an untested check fails the batch. The batch exits 1, the same as for a failed
run, including when `--filter` narrowed it to that one check. Its result line
says `FAILED`, or `nothing tested` when no check was tested at all. A line
after the skipped runs' reasons names the fixes: raise `--runs`, change the
setup until runs reach the check, or delete the check. A run count small enough
for every run of a check to be skipped, `--runs 1` while iterating for one,
fails the same way. A `--seed` replay of a skipped run is not a batch: it
prints the run's reason and detail, and exits 0. This is the harness's own
fixture pair, a check that passes and one that always gives up, over six runs:

```text
skipped runs hit a fault before reaching the check, so they prove nothing either way:
  always_gives_up — never tested: every run was skipped
    6  the fixture always gives up (first: --seed 0x3071c63893f2dcbd)
a check that tested nothing proves nothing, so the batch fails: the reasons above say where its runs stopped. Raise --runs, change the setup until runs reach the check (charpente_sim::drive runs the faulted phase, the recovery and the re-sends), or delete the check.

these checks draw no inputs, so every run tested the same case — draw them with sim.input() (GUIDE §8):
  always_passes

check always_passes ..... ok  (6 tested)
check always_gives_up ... untested  (0 tested, 6 skipped)

sim result: FAILED. 1 passed; 0 failed; 1 untested. Runs tested per check, in the order above: 6, 0 of 6.
rerun exactly this batch: charpente test --sim --master-seed 0x1234 --runs 6
```

"Reading a batch" below describes each line.

**Where the split is visible, and where it is not.** `charpente test --sim`
always prints it: the sim binary is `harness = false`, so nothing captures its
output. The platform's own internal suites print a line of their own —

```text
charpente-sim [replication]: 38 of 128 universes reached a verdict, 90 gave up
```

— but that one goes to stderr from inside a libtest `#[test]`, so **a passing
`cargo test` swallows it.** Add `-- --nocapture` to see it (a failing test
shows it either way). So do not expect a CI run to surface give-ups; go
looking for them. The one exception is a test in which every universe gave up,
which fails, as an untested check fails a batch:

```text
charpente-sim [wal]: every universe gave up, so this test adjudicated nothing over 64 universes and fails. Raise its universe count, or change the workload until universes reach a verdict.
```

A `CHARPENTE_SEED` replay of one universe that gave up still passes, as a
`--seed` replay of a skipped run exits 0.

Read that example line the right way round: **38** of those 128 runs
adjudicated the property and **90** did not. It is a real measurement from
`wal_corruption_on_the_primary_no_longer_loses_acked_records_with_a_follower`,
and it means 70% of that property's runs prove nothing — which was
invisible before the split was printed, and is the entire reason it is.

### A fault-free tail for an end-of-run check

A recovery under faults is four steps, and `charpente_sim::drive` runs them.
The app supplies how it builds itself on the run's sim and what to send;
"Driving the whole app in a sim" below has a compiled workload built on it.

```text
let drive = Drive::new(sim, build_app); // build_app: Fn(&Sim) -> io::Result<App>
let phase = drive.faulted_phase(&requests, Duration::from_secs(2))?; // steps 1 and 2
calm.store(true, Ordering::Relaxed); // a remote model's own switch stays yours
let Some(recovered) = drive.recover(&phase, Duration::from_secs(60))? else {
    return Ok(()); // given up by name
}; // steps 3 and 4
let snap = recovered.kernel.app().db().snapshot().map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
```

1. **Boot through fallout.** `faulted_phase` boots the app with
   `boot_through_fallout` (below), up to eight boots, restarting between
   them as a supervisor would. A first boot that never gets past fault
   fallout is no give-up: the phase is empty, and `recover` sends
   everything. In every step, fault fallout means `is_fault_fallout(t) ||
   clock_fallout(sim, t)`.
2. **A faulted phase.** The requests, each an offset from the instant the app
   booted and in arrival order, run through one kernel under the run's faults
   until nothing is left inside the horizon. An offset past the horizon
   panics before anything runs, since the phase would never send that
   request, naming the last offset, the horizon and the fix: raise the
   horizon past the last request, or space the requests closer. An offset
   equal to the horizon is sent. The kernel gets the sim's
   outbound handle exactly when the app declares hosts. Fault fallout ends
   the phase: it is the crash under test, never a give-up and never a skip,
   and the answers already recorded stand. `phase.answers[i]` is request
   `i`'s `(status, body)`, or `None` when it went unanswered;
   `phase.status(i)` is its status.
3. **A tail free of per-call faults; a drawn provider outage runs to its
   end.** `recover` power-cycles (`sim.restart()`), quiesces the disk and
   clock faults, and the outbound faults when the app declares hosts. A drawn
   `outbound_outage` is not a roll, so it refuses its host until its own end.
   It waits out a clock refusal, boots, and runs the kernel to `tail` past the
   instant it booted, so due jobs, retries and cron drain, then on until the
   app's outbound work settles (below). A remote model's own switches, such
   as `modelled_remote`'s `calm`, stay in your code: flip them between the two
   calls.
4. **Re-send what went unanswered.** Every request whose answer is `None`,
   or a 5xx whose body is fault fallout, is sent again at the tail's start. A
   4xx, or a handler's own 5xx, is the app's answer and is not re-sent.
   Fallout is read from the body's text (`is_fault_fallout`), so a handler's
   own 5xx whose message reads as fallout ("injected", say) is re-sent too.
   The re-sent answers come back in `recovered.resent`, apart from the
   phase's, because a request that went unanswered may or may not have
   landed. So a claim about acked requests reads `phase.status(i) ==
   Some(200)`, and a check that wants the two merged merges them itself
   (`examples/drawn_inputs.rs`). A re-sent request lands after everything the
   faulted phase ran, not at its offset, so work its offset came before may
   already have run by then: a cancel drawn ahead of a reminder's due time
   that went unanswered can land after the reminder was sent. A check that
   judges when a request took effect treats a re-sent one (a `Some` in
   `recovered.resent`) as landing after the phase, since `Recovered` gives no
   instant for the tail's start.

**The tail settles outbound work.** After the run to `tail`, `recover` reads
the store and the wall time the kernel judges due times by: the greater of the
clock's reading and the kernel's held wall, which the kernel's open seeds from
the store's recorded time, so it can sit up to the 60 s clock tolerance above
the clock. It stops when every Pending outbound row is not yet due: no attempt
made, and a due time after that wall time, as a delayed job (§6) or a re-issue
scheduled later is. Any other Pending outbound row extends the tail: the
kernel runs again, as far as the most, over those rows, of the row's wait (to
its due time; none once it is due) or the drawn outage's remaining length,
whichever is longer, plus its job's declared timeout plus 1 s, and the store
is read again. Idle virtual time costs a wake-up, so a retry's wait costs at
most one extension and so does a drawn outage, however long: through a 4 h
outage a `retry(max = 12)` job takes two extensions, not 17 hours of work, and
no app sizes `tail` to its retry policy. An `on_dead` hook that enqueues a fresh job
on every Dead, or re-issues at once, makes a chain that ends when the outage
ends: through a 4 h outage two such chains on the default policy take one
extension, and one on `crates/outbound-fixture`'s `ChargeOrder` policy (eight
attempts in 63.5 s) takes two. The extensions stop at
`charpente_sim::drive::TAIL_SETTLE_CAP`, 256, with work still Pending, and the
run fails by name (below). No extension runs past the year 2262: a retry whose
doubling backoff grew past it stays Pending, and once one extension has run to
that limit the run fails at once, by its own text naming the job's due time
(below), rather than at the end of the u64 nanosecond range, where the kernel
stops on its daily sweep's overflow. An app with no host has no outbound row,
so it is never extended. `recovered.extensions` counts the extensions, and
`recovered.wall` is the wall time the tail stopped at, the time to pass
`outbound_settles_by_horizon` ("The checkers", below).

**Coverage for a Dead path.** Last, `recover` calls `mark_dead_coverage`
("The checkers"), which marks `dead:<job>` once per Dead row, whatever the
job's stance: `dead:SendReceipt` for a `SendReceipt` job. Require it to hold
that the job's Dead path ran, as `crates/outbound-fixture`'s sim does:
`orders_charge_once require ["outbound:checked", "outbound-fixture:re-called",
"dead:SendReceipt"]`. A batch in which no run takes the job to Dead fails on
the key, naming it, so a Dead path that the sim never exercised cannot read
as tested. The tail extends only for outbound work, so a pure `on_dead` job
whose retries outlast `tail` is bounded by `tail` as before: size `tail` to
its policy before you require its `dead:` key. An outbound job on the default
policy reaches Dead under the outage axis, which `charpente test --sim` draws
in a quarter of runs, half
of them long enough to exhaust the policy ("The outside world: remote
models", below), but only where its calls land inside the window. The window
opens in the first two seconds of virtual time, so a job called at once is
often answered before it opens: the fixture enqueues its receipt 2 s after the
order (`enqueue_after`), and measured `dead:SendReceipt` in 486 of 4096 runs,
against 35 of 500 when the receipt was sent at once. Measure a required key's
rate before you rely on it, and write the rate beside the `require`, as the
fixture does: a fresh `cargo test` batch is 256 runs.

**A route the tail re-sends must be safe to receive twice.** `Drive`
re-sends, and a request that went unanswered may have landed, so its re-send
is the second delivery a production client's retry makes. Answer a repeat of
the same request with what the first one did, and change nothing:
`modelled_remote`'s `place_order` answers an order that is already stored, and
the scaffold's `add_note` a note already stored with the same body. A request
that reuses the id with other content is a
conflict, not a repeat: refuse it with a 4xx that names the conflict and the
fix, as `add_note` does for a note that already holds another body. Judge
the answers, not only the end state: a guard whose `==` is turned into `!=`
refuses the retry and answers the conflict as added, and leaves the store as
it was, so the scaffold's three end-state checks pass it. Its fourth,
`a_resent_note_changes_nothing_and_a_conflict_is_refused`, posts one note
twice with one body and once with another and judges each answer. Key a
state transition (a pickup, a cancel) by an id of its own, such as `PUT
/pickups/:pickup_id` naming the hold in its body and stored as a row: under
`POST /holds/:id/pickup` a retry of a pickup that landed and a fresh pickup of
a hold already picked up are the same bytes, while with its own id a repeat
finds its row and answers alike and a new pickup of a hold no longer waiting
is refused. A route
that fails this is an app defect the re-send found, and its check goes red
with a seed. Fix the route: dropping below the helper to skip the re-send
hides the defect.

**Seed fixtures in the build closure, not in the phase.** A `Drive` phase is a
fixed request list, so a key one request mints cannot feed a later one, and
every setup request sent ahead of a claim's own writes pushes them later into
the faulted phase, where fewer get acked and more runs give up: so write the
fixtures (an `ApiKey` row in §5's storage form, whose raw key the requests
present, and the rows a claim only reads) in the closure `Drive::new` takes,
through `App::transact_for_tests`, each only when it is absent, since the
closure runs on every boot.

`recover` gives `Ok(None)` when the run gave up by name, on a boot refused for
durable corruption and on nothing else. Return `Ok(())` then. Its errors fail
the run:

- `the fault-free tail could not boot`: every boot met fault fallout, and
  nothing gave the run up.
- `the boot was refused for the clock a third time, after two waits past the
  instant each refusal named. …, so this is a platform clock defect, not the
  app's: report it with this run's seed. The third refusal: the fault-free tail
  could not boot: <refusal>`, from `wait_out_clock_refusal`. One wait always
  suffices, so a refusal that outlasts two is a charpente defect, and the run
  fails on it rather than hiding it as a skip.
- `the fault-free tail could not boot: <error>`: a boot, or the kernel's
  build, failed with an error of its own.
- `the fault-free tail's kernel stopped: <error>`, in the kernel's words.
- `the app calls <host>, but the workload registered no model for it — call
  sim.remote("<host>", …) before Drive::faulted_phase`, from either call.
- The cap: the tail ran 256 extensions, and an outbound job is still Pending
  and due. The text names the first such job by id:

<!-- pinned -->
```text
Drive::recover: the tail ran 256 extensions past its horizon and outbound job <id> ("<name>") is still Pending at attempt <a> — an outbound job settles, retries to Dead, or waits for a later due time; this one did none of them within the cap of 256, so fix the model of the host it calls, or give the job, and the chain its on_dead hook makes, a retry policy that ends
```

A drawn outage costs one extension however long it lasts, so the outage is
never what reaches the cap. A job still retrying after 256 extensions has a
remote that never answers it in time and a policy that has not run out (a model
that never answers, or a `max` in the thousands), or an `on_dead` hook that
starts a new link on every Dead against such a remote, so the chain never
ends. Fix the model, or give the job, and the chain its hook makes, a policy
that ends. A named job at attempt 0 was due and never dispatched, so it is a
row the kernel lost, not the app's: report it with the run's seed.

- The limit: an extension has run to the year 2262, its reach meeting or
  passing the limit, and an outbound job is still Pending and due. The text
  names the job due last and its due time, and comes before the cap:

<!-- pinned -->
```text
Drive::recover: outbound job <id> ("<name>") is still Pending at attempt <a>, due <due>, and the tail ran <n> extension<s>, the last to the year 2262, past which it does not run — a due time that far out means the job's retry backoff grew past the year 2262, so no run reaches its next attempt; give the job a retry policy that ends, a retry(max = …) small enough that it reaches Dead
